# Who may sign a release tag of this repository, in the format git's
# `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` reads (ssh-keygen(1), ALLOWED SIGNERS).
#
# `publish.yml`'s `build` job verifies every `v*` tag against this file and
# stops the release on one it cannot verify. README.md, "Verifying a release",
# is how anyone else checks one - and why this file is not the anchor on its
# own: it travels in the same repository as the tag it vouches for, so a reader
# compares its key with the signing keys GitHub lists for the account.
#
# The principal is the address the tags already carry as tagger, so this file
# publishes nothing the tags did not.
#
# ROTATION, edited in the same change that starts using the new key:
#   - RETIRED key: keep its line and add valid-before="YYYYMMDD", the day it
#     stopped signing. git checks a key against the tag's own date, so the tags
#     it signed keep verifying. Deleting the line instead un-verifies all of them.
#   - COMPROMISED key: delete its line. A tag's date is whatever its signer
#     wrote, so valid-before cannot fence off a stolen key; the tags it signed
#     stop verifying, which is the honest state for them.
